    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "When complex numbers are multiplied, each part of one of the numbers gets distributed to the other one. This means that we'll always see the same pattern:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\((a+bi)(c+di)=ac+adi+bci+bdi^2\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can use the fact that \\(i^2=\\text-1\\) to rearrange this and make it easier to see the real part and the imaginary part of the result.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\((a+bi)(c+di)=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Every time we multiply complex numbers, the result is not only a complex number, but it's a specific complex number that comes from combining the parts of the numbers we started with in a specific way. If \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) are the real parts…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A number in the complex plane. It can be written as \\(a + bi\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers and \\(i^2 = \\text-1\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L15-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "15.2: Info Gap: What Was Multiplied?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A number on the imaginary number line. It can be written as \\(bi\\), where \\(b\\) is a real number and \\(i^2 = \\text-1\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l15.rich-lesson-v2.json",
